{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795358407,"nonce":"uV1UskLd/VL5Ln3L7A2fjJYp2bA=","title":"The ash_std unit suite fails or stalls when run in parallel on a loaded machine","message":"Observed on the Mac while checking another change, load average about 9 from other builds. Running the ash_std test binary directly ten times on the committed tree (no local change), each under a 90 second limit:\n- gc::tests::gc_reporting_prims_keep_their_documented_contract failed in 10 of 10 runs (the assertion on COLLECT_ORIGIN after hlp_gc_major).\n- socket::tests::loopback_stream_round_trip, gc::alloc_meter::tests::the_totals_count_another_threads_open_region_and_keep_it_when_the_thread_ends and fiber::host_wait_tests::a_timed_lock_wait_serves_the_watches_on_the_program_thread_and_nowhere_else each failed in some runs.\n- 7 of the 10 runs printed no final summary within 90 seconds.\n\nAt lower load the same binary passed (184 passed in about 2 seconds, one run), and each failing test passes when run alone. CI's test job passes.\n\nOne run, with a since-removed test that registered its thread and forced two major collections, stayed stuck for about 10 minutes at 0% CPU. A sample showed maps::hi64_tests::a_dropped_map_gives_back_its_malloc_index waiting in gc::stop_mutator_world (gc.rs, the condvar wait) with no other test thread alive, so the registry held an entry for a thread that was gone. Not reproduced on the committed tree and not root-caused.\n\nThe tests share one process-wide collector and COLLECT_ORIGIN, and several of them force collections, so they interfere under parallelism. Options: run the collector-forcing tests serially, or move them to their own process.","files":null}]}